/*
  SiteSearchVerse — the line the ⌕'s sheet rests on.

  ⚠ ONE COMPONENT, TWO STATES, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE REASON IT IS A FILE. The
  sheet opens on it, and `/search-panel` renders it again in two places: below
  `MIN_SEARCH`, and under the sentence that says a search found nothing. Three
  renderings of one verse is how one of them quietly becomes a different verse.

  ⚠ IT READS DIFFERENTLY IN EACH, WHICH IS THE POINT RATHER THAN AN ACCIDENT.
  On an empty field the first half is an invitation. Under "nothing matches" the
  second half is what just happened. The same sentence does both jobs, so
  nothing has to be written twice or explained.

  The wording is the NASB's, which is the rendering that keeps *"give up as
  lost"* — the ESV and NRSV both say "lose", which is the weaker line.
*/
---

<div class="ss-verse">
  <p class="ss-verse__line">A time to search, and a time to give up as lost.</p>
  <p class="ss-verse__cite">Ecclesiastes 3:6</p>
</div>
